WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.435 --> 00:00:06.563
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:09.966
We are now ready to learn how dynamic
programming algorithms

00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:14.050
work for finding optimal paths
in the graphs.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:15.570
Let's start from this graph.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:19.360
And let's try to figure out how to move in
this graph

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:25.930
to reach their sink with the maximum
number of attractions.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.440
At every step, there are only two
decisions to make.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.190
Either to move vertically or move
horizontally,

00:00:32.190 --> 00:00:33.840
south or east.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.300
So, to arrive to the final destination we

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.980
can either arrive by moving south or
moving east.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:44.720
And let's define a variable, which is

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:52.120
SouthOrEast(i,j) as the length of
the longest path from the initial node

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:56.950
node (0,0) to node (i,j) in the grid.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:03.210
And, of course, we can immediately write a
simple recursive program for

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:10.220
computing south or east for (n,m),
for the final destination vertex.

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:17.200
It is simply the minimum of
SouthOrEast of (n-1,m)

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.830
plus the weight of the vertical edge into the
final node.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:28.442
Or SouthOrEast(n,m-1) +
weight of horizontal edge

00:01:29.278 --> 00:01:34.210
into the final node.
And as soon as we analyzed it, there

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:39.180
is a very simple recursive algorithm for
computing the optimal path.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.530
To node (i,j) and the algorithm is called
SouthOrEast

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:49.452
and the only thing this algorithm does,
is to compute

00:01:49.452 --> 00:01:51.430
alternatives between two scenarios.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:58.860
Moving to node (i,j) using the vertical edge or
using to node (i,j) using the horizontal edge.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.140
Using this algorithm will solve our problem.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:07.420
If you watch me when I explain the change
problem, you will figure out

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:12.620
that this algorithm, while being
correct, will take enormous time to finish.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.790
Because we will be making recursive

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:20.600
calls many, many times, for a fixed value
of i and j.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.907
And therefore, we need to come up with
something better, and let's try

00:02:25.907 --> 00:02:31.304
to apply the dynamic program idea
for traveling in this grid.

00:02:31.304 --> 00:02:36.380
Well, the optimal pass to reach node
zero, of course

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.970
we will see only zero attractions, so the
score in this node will be zero

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.630
The score in this node will be 1 because

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.960
there is only one way to reach this node by
moving vertically.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.560
The optimal score to reach this node will
be 5,

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.820
which is 1 plus 4, because there is no
other alternative.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:00.860
And as a result, we can compute all values
we described the lengths of the longest path.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:06.140
For the first row and the first column on
this graph.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.110
Now something interesting starts.

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:13.390
What is the best way to reach this node in
the graph, the node (1,1).

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:17.800
Should we move to this node using
horizontal or vertical?

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.630
South or east?
Well, if we move

00:03:22.772 --> 00:03:27.444
horizontally into this node, then the
length of the path will be 1 plus 3,

00:03:27.444 --> 00:03:31.610
and if we move vertically, the length of
the path will be 3 plus 0.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:37.880
Of course, we choose to move horizontally,
because 1 plus 3 is larger than 3 plus 0.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:42.984
And we know afterwards that
the optimal path to this node has length 4.

00:03:42.984 --> 00:03:48.190
And for the future, we represent the edge

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:52.910
horizontal edge that we use, to
reach this node,

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.370
as the bold edge, and we will see later why
it is important.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:57.580
How about this edge?

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.000
The same argument, and we decide to move
to this node

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.660
by using a vertical edge in this case.
And we'll continue further and further.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.470
Let us try one more time.
What about this node?

00:04:09.470 --> 00:04:13.223
How should we arrive to this node?
In this case we compare, 4 plus 2

00:04:13.223 --> 00:04:15.140
with 5 plus 2.

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:16.988
5 plus 2 is larger than 4 plus 2

00:04:16.988 --> 00:04:20.240
and therefore, we choose to arrive to this
node

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.740
by moving along vertical edge.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:28.230
Therefore, the recurrence for computing
the longest paths is the following.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:33.114
We define S(i,j) as the length of the
longest path

00:04:33.114 --> 00:04:38.394
from the initial node to (i,j),
and S(i,j) is simply

00:04:38.394 --> 00:04:44.598
the maximum of S(i-1,j) + the weight
of the vertical edge

00:04:44.598 --> 00:04:51.320
or S(i,j-1) + the weight of
the horizontal edge.

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:57.161
And after we finish this, then in the
programming procedure, we will

00:04:57.161 --> 00:05:03.596
fill the value of longest paths
for every node and we'll learn that

00:05:03.596 --> 00:05:09.070
the optimal path to the final destination
has length 34.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:14.440
When we are doing this, please note that
in some cases, instead

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:19.730
of making decision South or East, we will
make a decision South and East.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:23.420
For example, in this particular node, we
are comparing

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:27.000
22 plus 0, which is equal to 20 plus 2.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.430
Which means that both

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:34.280
horizontal and vertical edges entering into
this vertex are in bold.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:38.060
And now, I hope it will become clear

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:42.520
why we were retaining information about
both edges.

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:49.200
Because 34, the number in the last node,
tells us the length of the longest path.

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:55.260
But it doesn't tell us how this longest
path was traversing the graph.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:01.110
But with both edges, we can figure out because
we know that we arrive to the last

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:06.680
node marked by 34 by moving along
a horizontal bold edge, here it is.

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:11.340
We arrive in the node labeled by 32.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:15.060
Once again, by moving by horizontal edge,
and we arrive

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:20.180
to this node labeled by 30 by moving along
a vertical edge.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:25.610
We continue backtracking in this way, and
sooner or later, we will arrive

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:31.610
in the initial node, and the blue path
shown on the slide is actually

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:37.560
an optimal path from the initial to the final
destination in this graph.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.320
And we now move from describing how

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.380
dynamic programming works in a simple
Manhattan grid

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:49.690
to describing how it works
in arbitrary alignment graphs.

